Question 1: Squaring a Sorted Array (Two-Pointer) 
Anjali is processing seismic intensity readings stored in a non-decreasing sorted array. The readings can contain negative values (representing reverse-phase wave intensities) as well as positive values. To calculate the kinetic energy of these waves, she needs to square each value and return a new array containing the squares, sorted in non-decreasing order. 
She must perform this operation in 𝑂(𝑁) time using the two-pointer technique: 
1.	Initialize a left pointer at index 0 and a right pointer at index N - 1. 
2.	Compare the absolute values of arr[left] and arr[right]. 
3.	Square the larger absolute value and place it at the end of the output array (starting from index N - 1 and moving backwards). 
4.	Move the corresponding pointer (left inward or right inward) and repeat until the output array is filled. 
•	The first line of input consists of an integer 𝑁 representing the number of readings. 
•	The second line of input consists of 𝑁 space-separated integers representing the sorted wave intensities (can contain negative numbers). 
•	Print a single line of 𝑁 space-separated integers representing the sorted squared values. 
•	1 ≤ 𝑁 ≤ 10000 
•	−104 ≤ 𝑎𝑟𝑟[𝑖] ≤ 104 
•	The input array is sorted in non-decreasing order. 
Input 1: codeText 
5 
-4 -1 0 3 10 Output 1: codeText 0 1 9 16 100 Input 2: codeText 
4 
-7 -3 2 3 Output 2: codeText 
4 9 9 49 

Aim
To write a C program to find the square of each element in a sorted array and display the squared values in sorted order using the two-pointer method.

 

Pseudocode

Start
Read N
Read N elements into the array
Set left = 0
Set right = N - 1
Set index = N - 1
While left <= right
Set leftValue = array[left]
If leftValue < 0
leftValue = -leftValue
End If
Set rightValue = array[right]
If rightValue < 0
rightValue = -rightValue
End If
If leftValue > rightValue
result[index] = array[left] * array[left]
left = left + 1
Else
result[index] = array[right] * array[right]
right = right - 1
End If
index = index - 1
End While
Print the elements of the result array
End

C Program Code
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int n;
    int arr[100];
    int result[100];
    int left, right, index;
    int leftValue, rightValue;
    int i;
    printf("Enter the number of elements: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    printf("Enter the sorted array elements:\n");
    for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
    }
    left = 0;
    right = n - 1;
    index = n - 1;
    while(left <= right)
    {
        leftValue = arr[left];
        if(leftValue < 0)
        {
            leftValue = -leftValue;
        }
        rightValue = arr[right];
        if(rightValue < 0)
        {
            rightValue = -rightValue;
        }
        if(leftValue > rightValue)
        {
            result[index] = arr[left] * arr[left];
            left++;
        }
        else
        {
            result[index] = arr[right] * arr[right];
            right--;
        }
  index--;
    }
    printf("Sorted squared array:\n");
    for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", result[i]);
    }
    return 0;
}

