Question 3: Grouping Active Sectors (Fixed-Size Sliding Window) 
Sam is working on a flash storage defragmentation algorithm. The sectors of the storage drive are represented by a binary array of size 𝑁, where 1 represents an active data sector and 0 represents an empty sector. To optimize read operations, Sam wants to group all active sectors (1s) together in a contiguous block. 
He wants to find the minimum number of swaps required to bring all 1s together. This can be solved in 𝑂(𝑁) time using a sliding window: 
1.	Count the total number of 1s in the array. Let this count be 𝐶. If 𝐶 is 0 or 1, no swaps are needed. 
2.	Find a contiguous subarray (window) of size 𝐶 that contains the maximum number of 1s. 
3.	The minimum number of swaps required is then 𝐶 minus the maximum number of 1s found in any window of size 𝐶. 
•	The first line of input consists of an integer 𝑁 representing the number of sectors. 
•	The second line of input consists of 𝑁 space-separated binary values (0 or 1). 
•	Print a single integer representing the minimum swaps required in the format: Min Swaps: X. 
•	1 ≤ 𝑁 ≤ 10000 
•	arr[i] is either 0 or 1. 
Input 1: codeText 
6 
1	0 1 0 1 0 
Output 1: 
codeText 
Min Swaps: 1
 Input 2: 
codeText 
5 
1 1 1 0 0 
Output 2: 
codeText Min Swaps: 0 






Aim

To write a C program to find the minimum number of swaps required to bring all the 1's together in a binary array using the sliding window method.
Pseudocode

Start
Read N
Read the array elements
Set count = 0
For i = 0 to N - 1
If array[i] == 1
count = count + 1
If count is 0 or 1
Print "Min Swaps: 0"
Stop
Set one = 0
For i = 0 to count - 1
If array[i] == 1
one = one + 1
Set max = one
For i = count to N - 1
If array[i] == 1
one = one + 1
If array[i - count] == 1
one = one - 1
If one > max
